{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/934391-turning-morality-the-statistic-in-the-other-direction","id":934391,"title":"Turning Morality (the Statistic) in the Other Direction","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-20T04:13:58-07:00","id":934391,"title":"Turning Morality (the Statistic) in the Other Direction","content":"In 1e, Morality was meant to reflect how moral a character actually was - but losing the main drawback for losing Morality was insanity, an odd combination which probably lead the writers to make it a purer measure of mental stability in the second edition.<br /> <br /> But what if you wanted to do the opposite and design a Morality that measured exactly that and nothing else? How would you do it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not the <a href=\"https://www.theoi.com/Daimon/Alastor.html\" target=\"_blank\">daimon</a>, just a namesake.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/858-alastor","name":"Alastor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"62"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-20T04:55:58-07:00","id":934403,"title":"","content":"If you want to make morality personal: redesign the integrity questions to it becomes a moral code rather than a measure of your sense of self<br /> If you want to make morality universal: get rid of the insanity bit and just have conditions like guilty for anything but an exceptional success on a morality roll.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6365-fumus","name":"Fumus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6365_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"310"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-20T08:34:17-07:00","id":934505,"title":"","content":"What exactly does it mean to measure morality? Do you mean, &quot;How long does it take to unburden yourself in a confessional&quot;, or...?<br /> <br /> I'd recommend reading the GMC Rules Update for the explanation of why CofD transitioned from Morality to Integrity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-20T08:56:24-07:00","id":934514,"title":"","content":"Giving everyone a &quot;morality&quot; trait forces you down one of two paths:<br /> <br /> First, you'll end up having to decide on the universal morality of your gaming world, which may sit poorly with significant portions of the player base (exactly what happened with 1e).<br /> <br /> Second, you'll end up having a &quot;design your own&quot; morality system, but when it's just about morality there's very little to stop this from getting very stupid in play. Players that choose moral codes that are extremely permissive for the play styles common among CofD games (purposefully or incidentally) get off ease compared to those that don't; because you can't create a real balanced approach to this.<br /> <br /> Hence, as noted, 2e as just moved away from morality as the metric in question. Morality can impact things (2e Humanity technically measures your connection to your former human self-identity, not your morality, but poor moral choices are harder on detachment rolls because they're blurry between &quot;acting human&quot; by being fallible, and &quot;losing yourself to the Beast&quot; because you're doing immoral things that make you think of yourself as less human), but being the actual metric doesn't really provide anything positive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-20T09:03:04-07:00","id":934518,"title":"","content":"I was actually just thinking about something like this. Although for me it wouldn't apply to humans, rather it could be the Integrity equivalent for a splat that would make sense. Like a Paladin or other 'Righteous' supernatural. Thus the morality would be objective from the point of view of that spat but not universal for anyone else.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T04:04:25-07:00","id":934985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/934391-turning-morality-the-statistic-in-the-other-direction?p=934514#post934514\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Giving everyone a &quot;morality&quot; trait forces you down one of two paths:<br /> <br /> First, you'll end up having to decide on the universal morality of your gaming world, which may sit poorly with significant portions of the player base (exactly what happened with 1e).<br /> <br /> Second, you'll end up having a &quot;design your own&quot; morality system, but when it's just about morality there's very little to stop this from getting very stupid in play. Players that choose moral codes that are extremely permissive for the play styles common among CofD games (purposefully or incidentally) get off ease compared to those that don't; because you can't create a real balanced approach to this.<br /> <br /> Hence, as noted, 2e as just moved away from morality as the metric in question. Morality can impact things (2e Humanity technically measures your connection to your former human self-identity, not your morality, but poor moral choices are harder on detachment rolls because they're blurry between &quot;acting human&quot; by being fallible, and &quot;losing yourself to the Beast&quot; because you're doing immoral things that make you think of yourself as less human), but being the actual metric doesn't really provide anything positive.</div> </div> </div> </div>My preference would be for the group around the table to agree on one moral standard to apply to the mortals in their game world. As friends, they would stand a better chance of reaching an agreement (or at least a workable compromise) than a team trying to make the same decisions on behalf of Onyx Path game players everywhere. Hopefully, the compromise process would cut down on the &quot;morality of whatever I was going to do anyway&quot; effect, since they will each have different plans for their characters. If not, then that would be all right, since they would all have agreed that that was how they wanted to play. The idea is to reinforce the themes the players and storyteller want to see in their game.<br /> <br /> Perhaps there could be a few options to choose from based on the ideas of different philosophies and cultures (<i>Dark Ages: Vampire </i>did a good job of this in my opinion), but fully subjective morality would be problematic (not philosophically, but practically). If you no longer look at something as immoral, does that mean that you've changed your moral code, or is your existing Morality just lower? <i>Hunter: the Vigil</i> handled the distinction rather well, but they needed the pre-existing universal Morality to handle divergences from it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not the <a href=\"https://www.theoi.com/Daimon/Alastor.html\" target=\"_blank\">daimon</a>, just a namesake.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/858-alastor","name":"Alastor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"62"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T04:26:44-07:00","id":934991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ElvesofZion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/934391-turning-morality-the-statistic-in-the-other-direction?p=934518#post934518\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was actually just thinking about something like this. Although for me it wouldn't apply to humans, rather it could be the Integrity equivalent for a splat that would make sense. Like a Paladin or other 'Righteous' supernatural. Thus the morality would be objective from the point of view of that spat but not universal for anyone else.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's one use that makes sense.<br /> <br /> One thing about the original Morality that I liked was that replacing it underscored how different a supernatural group was from humanity. They might still care about goodness and decency, but they no longer were affected by them as deeply. It emphasized the monstrosity of the supernatural characters to place them at a remove from the usual yardstick of good and evil. If you leave humans with Integrity and give Morality to some other group, then it would produce a similar effect, but if the Morality you use matches up to your actual moral code, it becomes an open question which to group you would feel closer. It would make for an interesting game. To accomodate different points of view, what do you think of providing variations for each Y-type?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Not the <a href=\"https://www.theoi.com/Daimon/Alastor.html\" target=\"_blank\">daimon</a>, just a namesake.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/858-alastor","name":"Alastor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"62"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T07:49:27-07:00","id":935064,"title":"","content":"Maybe, it depends on the creature in question and how Morality is tied to their being and/or power set. I hadn't given much more thought to it then in my previous post.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T07:57:20-07:00","id":935067,"title":"","content":"In our samurai game, I've struggled with how to portray Honor. At first, I started with fairly standard 1-10 scale, but as I written and tested and written and tested and written and tested, I grew to dislike it. It was tiered, like Mage. In the end, I threw the number value out, and just turned the whole stat into a set of Conditions that change either by succeeding/failing on a roll provoked by partially-customizable* Code of Honor, or by resolving Conditions attached to it, and thus helping create desirable gameplay patterns that reinforce target fiction/setting. I also noticed that players are much less uncomfortable about going from &quot;Honorable&quot; to &quot;Honorless&quot; Condition than with dropping from 5 point in Honor Stat to 3 points in Honor Stat; second one is often perceived as some failure or punishment, while first one is less antagonizing, even if they are virtually the same.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">*It being a L5R game, you basically answered some breaking-point style questions to figure out your character's ideal of Honor, influenced that with Virtue / Vice, and added Clan modifiers, *then* check for circumstancial modifiers, like acting towards or against your Duty [with Duty being special kind of Aspiration, just like Mage's Obsession] that could make certain acts more or less honorable. While it got away from original idea of &quot;universal Honor to gauge them all&quot;, in the end we like it more, and it was a rare case where John Wick actually inspired me by his comment that &quot;if he was to design Honor system with his current knowledge and afterthought, he would give each Clan their own rules for Honor&quot;; for CofD style Samurai game of personal drama, figuring out what is your answer to the question of Honor feels as one of appropriate themes (personal (individual) idea of Honor versus needs of your (collectivistic) Duty being one of central ones)</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T12:02:58-07:00","id":935179,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Alastor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/934391-turning-morality-the-statistic-in-the-other-direction?p=934985#post934985\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My preference would be for the group around the table to agree on one moral standard to apply to the mortals in their game world. As friends, they would stand a better chance of reaching an agreement (or at least a workable compromise) than a team trying to make the same decisions on behalf of Onyx Path game players everywhere.</div> </div> </div> </div>Morality and ethics are really not something I think are good for most groups to have to hammer out before play.<br /> <br /> First, I don't imagine most groups actually wanting to hash this out just to play a game. Morality is a pretty heavy topic to have to wade through just to play.<br /> <br /> Second, these are things that can go from 0 to 11 in terms of heated debate almost instantly. Even people that agree on 99% of things can be incredible passionate about the morality of specific topics; and these can be very person and emotional. One of my old gaming groups essentially split in half because two people could no longer get along over whether it was OK to say positive things about Hitler at all - not as an excuse for the evil of his actions, but just that he did some things effectively/well - and you can't really tell a guy that was raised by Holocaust survivors to calm down about the whole Hitler thing, even if most of us agreed he was being unreasonable about it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Alastor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/934391-turning-morality-the-statistic-in-the-other-direction?p=934991#post934991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing about the original Morality that I liked was that replacing it underscored how different a supernatural group was from humanity.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is still pretty significant for Integrity though. Going from Integrity to any of the supernatural equivalents is pretty jarring. You can't just keeping going how you were going via Integrity, being changed changes how your choices impact you.<br /> <br /> A 2e werewolf can care all they want about goodness and decency, but they go from Integrity to Harmony, and it needs to slot in with the hunt or they're aiming for Harmony 10 and all the downsides therein; esp. the issue where their refusal to be &quot;bad&quot; and hunt things down with terrible might actually makes them more likely to flip out and kill innocent people.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}